.Dd November 5, 2026
.Dt ELOG 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm elog
.Nd subscribe to and read events from eventlog device
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl b Ar size
.Op Fl d
.Op Fl D
.Op Fl e
.Op Fl f Ar type
.Op Fl n
.Op Fl p
.Op Fl s
.Op Fl S Ar scenario
.Op Fl t
.Op Fl -delta-time
.Op Fl -duration Ar seconds
.Op Fl o Ar file | Cm dir= Ns Ar path
.Op Fl -roll-size Ar size
.Op Fl -roll-time Ar interval
.Op Fl -roll-prefix Ar name
.Op Fl -roll-keep Ar count
.Op Fl -gzip Op Ar level
.Op Fl N
.Op Fl U
.Op Fl -symbol-mode Ar mode
.Op Fl c Ar provider Op Ar level Op Ar keywords
.Op Fl c Ar provider Op Ar level Op Ar keywords
.Op ...
.Op Fl h
.Nm
.Fl r Ar file
.Op Fl d
.Op Fl e
.Op Fl n
.Op Fl p
.Op Fl t
.Op Fl -delta-time
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
utility subscribes to events from one or more eventlog providers and displays
them in a formatted output on standard output or writes them to a file.
.Pp
The utility opens the single system-wide eventlog device at
.Pa /dev/eventlog
and sends subscription requests for the specified providers.
.Pp
The eventlog framework is host-global and is not exposed to jailed
processes.
The
.Nm
utility must be run from the host
.Pq Va prison0 ;
running it from inside a jail fails because
.Xr open 2
on
.Pa /dev/eventlog
returns
.Er EPERM .
.Pp
.Nm
decodes events at runtime using a
.Dq decode blob
that each provider registers with the eventlog framework (see
.Xr eventlog 9 ) ;
it contains no compiled-in, per-provider decoding logic.
Blobs are fetched from the live kernel when rendering text, and may optionally
be embedded into a binary capture with
.Fl E
so it can be decoded later without the originating kernel.
.Pp
When a subscription matches a user-space provider
.Pq see Xr eventlog_user 3 ,
the kernel allocates, sizes, and reclaims the per-stream receive buffers on
demand;
.Nm
declares nothing about them.
The total is bounded by the
.Va kern.eventlog.max_user_streams
sysctl, an administrative ceiling; events dropped because that ceiling is
reached are counted in the dropped-event total reported by
.Fl s .
.Pp
The options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Fl c Ar provider Op Ar level Op Ar keywords
.It Fl -capture Ar provider Op Ar level Op Ar keywords
Subscribe to events from the specified provider.
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Ar provider
The name of the eventlog provider (e.g., "tcp").
This argument is required.
.It Ar level
Optional log level.
Can be specified as a case-insensitive string
(NONE, ERROR, WARN, INFO, VERBOSE, TRACE)
or as a numeric value (0\(en5).
Defaults to VERBOSE if not specified.
TRACE captures all events including internal algorithmic details.
VERBOSE excludes TRACE-level events.
.It Ar keywords
Optional keyword filter.
Can be specified as a hexadecimal mask (e.g., 0x3F) or as pipe-delimited
keyword names (e.g., CC|RX|TX), which the shell requires quoting
.Pq Ql "CC|RX|TX" .
Keyword names are provider-specific and case-insensitive.
A name prefixed with
.Sq ^
is negated: its keyword is excluded from the mask instead of added.
A list of only negated names starts from all keywords and removes the
named ones (e.g., ^SAMPLE|^SAMPLE_HIST captures everything except SAMPLE
and SAMPLE_HIST); when positive and negated names are mixed, the negated
names are subtracted from the positive ones.
Names resolve against the provider's schema, so a provider that has not
registered yet starts unfiltered and narrows when it appears, which may
capture a few of its earliest events outside the filter.
A provider that publishes no schema never narrows, so filter it by mask.
Defaults to 0xFFFFFFFF (all keywords) if not specified.
.El
.It Fl S Ar scenario
.It Fl -scenario Ar scenario
Subscribe using a named capture preset.
Presets can be combined with additional
.Fl c
subscriptions, and multiple presets can be given in one
.Fl S
argument separated by
.Ql | ,
or by repeating
.Fl S .
The following scenarios are defined:
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Cm cpu
Equivalent to
.Fl c Cm cpu Fl c Cm process Fl c Cm symbols Fl D Fl U .
Captures the providers needed to produce a CPU stack trace, including
dump-state identity events and user-mode symbol resolution.
.It Cm net , Cm network
Equivalent to
.Fl c Cm if Fl c Cm tcp Ar SAMPLE|SAMPLE_HIST Fl D .
Captures interface events and TCP sample / sample-histogram snapshots.
.El
.It Fl b Ar size
.It Fl -buffer-size Ar size
Set per-CPU buffer size.
The size can be specified as bytes, or with K/M/G suffix for Kilobytes,
Megabytes, or Gigabytes.
Examples: 65536, 64K, 1M, 256K.
Valid range: 4KB to 1GB per CPU.
Default: 512KB.
.It Fl d
.It Fl -date
Show the full date in timestamps.
When enabled, timestamps are displayed in the format
.Li YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS.uuuuuu
instead of the default
.Li HH:MM:SS.uuuuuu .
.It Fl D
.It Fl -dump-state
Request providers dump their current state when subscribing.
This causes providers to emit initial state events (e.g., connection parameters,
current congestion window) immediately upon subscription.
See
.Fl -roll-time
for a re-triggerable form of this same replay, used automatically at each
rolled file's boundary regardless of whether
.Fl D
was given.
.It Fl U
.It Fl -user-symbols
Resolve user-mode functions from streamed
.Li symbols.USER_FILE
events, emitting
.Li symbols.USER_SYMBOL_RANGE
events (one per function).
Cannot be used with
.Fl r .
.It Fl L
.It Fl -embed-source-lines
Resolve each unique PC in sampled stacks against on-disk DWARF and emit
.Li symbols.SOURCE_FILE
/
.Li symbols.SOURCE_LINE
events so offline tools can annotate source without a matching local ELF.
Looks for debug info next to each binary, under
.Pa /usr/lib/debug ,
and via
.Li .gnu_debuglink .
Kernel source files are stamped with the firmware commit parsed from
.Li kern.version
when it names a PR build
.Pq e.g. Li "pull/5915-n8635-a36b8d97a3a7" .
The exit summary breaks misses down by cause and lists binaries that
produced no lines.
Cannot be used with
.Fl r .
.It Fl -symbol-mode Ar mode
Controls symbol table emission in binary captures.
.Cm inline
(the default) embeds full
.Li symbols.SYMBOL_RANGE
and
.Li symbols.USER_SYMBOL_RANGE
rows.
.Cm refs
embeds identity rows plus one
.Li symbols.SYMBOL_PACK_REF
per binary carrying a GNU build-id, leaving function ranges to the
symbol catalog; binaries without a build-id stay inline.
Implies
.Fl U .
.It Fl e
.It Fl -event-name
Show the event name (e.g., IN, OUT, SESSION_CREATE) in square brackets
after the session ID in each output line.
.It Fl f Ar type
.It Fl -format Ar type
Rename per-session output files with connection metadata.
The only supported
.Ar type
is
.Cm tcp ,
which renames files with TCP connection metadata.
Files are named using the format
.Ar log_id Ns _ Ns Ar local_port Ns _ Ns Ar remote_ip Ns _ Ns Ar remote_port Ns .elog .
Requires
.Fl o Cm dir= Ns Ar path .
.It Fl n
.It Fl -event-number
Print a serial event number at the beginning of each output line.
Events are numbered starting from 1.
.It Fl o Ar file
.It Fl -output Ar file
Write output to the specified file in binary format.
See
.Xr elog 5
for a description of the binary file format.
Uses buffered I/O for efficiency.
Standard output always uses formatted text.
.It Fl o Cm dir= Ns Ar path
Write one binary file per session under
.Ar path .
Each session gets its own file, named by session ID (or by TCP metadata
when combined with
.Fl f Cm tcp ) .
The directory is created if it does not exist.
.It Fl -roll-size Ar size
Enable rolling output: instead of one file per session, write a single
continuous stream to
.Fl o Cm dir= Ns Ar path ,
rolling to a fresh file once the current one reaches
.Ar size
uncompressed bytes.
The size accepts a K/M/G suffix (e.g.,
.Li 100M ) .
When
.Fl -gzip
is not used, size the roll to the uncompressed bytes needed to reach the
desired object size if a downstream step will gzip each file separately.
Requires
.Fl o Cm dir= Ns Ar path
and is incompatible with
.Fl f Cm tcp .
.It Fl -roll-time Ar interval
Enable rolling output and roll to a fresh file every
.Ar interval ,
determined by event timestamp, so that no file ever spans an interval
boundary.
The
.Ar interval
accepts an
.Cm s Ns / Ns Cm m Ns / Ns Cm h Ns / Ns Cm d
suffix (seconds, minutes, hours, days); a bare number is seconds.
Boundaries are aligned to the UTC epoch, so an interval that evenly divides
a day
.Pq e.g. Cm 15m , Cm 1h , Cm 6h , Cm 1d
yields calendar-aligned files that map one-to-one onto time-partitioned
tables downstream
.Pq Cm 1h No reproduces hourly files .
May be combined with
.Fl -roll-size ,
in which case the file rolls on whichever limit is reached first; the
interval boundary always forces a roll.
Files are bucketed by event time, so a file for interval N contains only
events whose timestamps fall in N.
An interval's file is finalized when an event for a later interval
arrives, when the wall clock passes the interval's end plus a grace of a
quarter interval (at most 10 seconds), or on exit.
An event that arrives for an interval after its file was finalized opens
that interval's next sequence file.
An interval with no events produces no file.
Each time-interval boundary re-triggers a dump-state replay on the
existing subscriptions, so every rolled file is independently decodable
(process/symbol/TCP-stack identity events land in each interval's file, not
just the first) regardless of whether
.Fl D
was passed.
A
.Fl -roll-size
seq-split within the same interval does not re-trigger this replay.
Requires
.Fl o Cm dir= Ns Ar path .
.It Fl -roll-prefix Ar name
Filename prefix for rolled files.
Rolled files are named
.Ar name Ns - Ns Ar YYYY-MM-DDTHHMMSS Ns Cm Z Ns - Ns Ar seq Ns .elog ,
where the timestamp is the file's UTC interval start (or first-event time
when only
.Fl -roll-size
is used) and
.Ar seq
distinguishes multiple files within the same interval (from
.Fl -roll-size ) .
With
.Fl -roll-time ,
the last finalized file's interval and
.Ar seq
are kept in
.Pa . Ns Ar name Ns .seq
so a restarted
.Nm
continues the interval's sequence instead of reusing a name whose file was
already moved away.
The in-progress file is written as a
.Pa .tmp.*
dotfile and renamed to its final name on close, so a directory watcher
only observes complete captures.
Defaults to
.Cm eventlog .
.It Fl -roll-keep Ar count
Retain at most
.Ar count
finalized files in the output directory, deleting the oldest whenever a new
file is finalized.
Only files matching this run's rolled-file naming convention are considered,
so the in-progress
.Pa .tmp.*
file and unrelated files are never removed; files left by a previous run are
counted and eligible for deletion.
By default no limit is applied.
Requires
.Fl -roll-size
or
.Fl -roll-time .
.It Fl -gzip Op Ar level
Gzip each finalized binary capture after close.
.Ar level
is a zlib compression level from 1 (fastest) to 9 (smallest); when the
option is given without
.Ar level ,
the default is 6.
By default captures are left uncompressed.
Requires binary output
.Pq Fl o Ar file or Fl o Cm dir= Ns Ar path .
The compressed bytes are written to a
.Pa .tmp.*
file in the same directory and renamed to the final
.Pa .elog.gz
name when compression finishes.
In rolling mode, compression runs in a background worker so the main
event-drain loop stays unblocked; finalized rolled files are published as
.Pa .elog.gz
once compression completes.
.It Fl N
.It Fl -no-embed-decode
Skip embedding each captured provider's decode blob into the output file.
By default, binary output
.Pq Fl o Ar file or Fl o Cm dir= Ns Ar path
embeds each provider's decode blob so the capture can be decoded later on a
machine that does not run the originating kernel; this flag omits them,
producing a smaller file whose later decode
.Pq Fl r
requires the live kernel to still provide matching decode blobs.
Only meaningful for binary output; plain text output has nowhere to embed
blobs.
.It Fl p
.It Fl -providers
Print all registered provider names to stderr at the beginning of output,
before any events are displayed.
.It Fl r Ar file
.It Fl -read-binary Ar file
Read a binary file created with
.Fl o
and convert it to formatted text output.
See
.Xr elog 5
for a description of the binary file format.
The binary file header includes the total event count and a set of
drop counters (kernel and user-space).
If the filename ends in
.Pa .gz ,
the file is transparently decompressed using zlib.
This mode cannot be used with capture options.
Decoding requires a decode blob for each provider in the file.
Each provider is resolved by its content-addressed
.Va schema_id
.Pq see Xr elog 5 :
.Nm
uses the blob embedded in the file
.Pq see Fl E
if present, otherwise it fetches the blob with that
.Va schema_id
from the live kernel via
.Pa /dev/eventlog ,
and errors out if neither source has it.
Resolving by
.Va schema_id
rather than by provider name decodes the exact schema that produced each event,
even after a reboot reassigns provider ids or a newer same-named provider is
loaded.
Both the current version 3 format and the version 2 format, which differs
only in its header
.Pq see Xr elog 5 ,
are read.
The obsolete version 1 format, which predates
.Va schema_id ,
is not.
.It Fl s
.It Fl -stats
Print detailed statistics on exit, including provider count, the number
of events received, and any dropped events.
Statistics are printed to stderr.
Without this flag, no statistics are printed.
.It Fl t
.It Fl -relative-time
Show time relative to the first event in the trace.
Each output line is prefixed with a relative timestamp in the format
.Li +seconds.microseconds
(e.g.,
.Li +1.234567 ) .
.It Fl -delta-time
Show time elapsed since the previous event.
Each output line is prefixed with a delta timestamp in the format
.Li d Ns seconds.microseconds
(e.g.,
.Li d0.000015 ) .
Can be combined with
.Fl t .
.It Fl -duration Ar seconds
Self-exit after
.Ar seconds
seconds.
.Nm
raises
.Dv SIGALRM
internally and takes the same cleanup path as
.Dv SIGINT
or
.Dv SIGTERM :
binary output is flushed, the file header is updated with final
event/drop counts, and per-session files are closed and renamed.
A value of 0 disables the timer (the default), in which case
.Nm
runs until a signal is received.
Intended for scripted captures
.Pq Xr oca.py 1 Cm get Cm capture , cron jobs, ...
that want a fixed recording window without relying on an external
.Xr kill 1 .
.It Fl h
.It Fl -help
Display usage information and exit.
.El
.Pp
Multiple provider subscriptions can be specified by using multiple
.Fl c
flags, and
.Fl S
presets may be mixed with extra
.Fl c
captures
.Pq e.g. Fl S Cm cpu Fl c Cm tcp .
.Pp
The utility reads events continuously, blocking when no data is available.
Events are displayed with timestamp, CPU, thread ID, provider name, session ID,
and formatted event data.
When
.Fl n , Fl t ,
or
.Fl -delta-time
are enabled, their respective prefixes appear at the beginning of each line
before the CPU and thread fields.
.Pp
Statistics are only printed when the
.Fl s
flag is specified.
When enabled, statistics include the number of providers, events received,
and any dropped events.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
.Sh EXAMPLES
Subscribe to all TCP events at VERBOSE level with all keywords:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
elog -c tcp
.Ed
.Pp
Subscribe to TCP events at INFO level with all keywords:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
elog -c tcp INFO
.Ed
.Pp
Subscribe to TCP events at INFO level with specific keywords by name:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
elog -c tcp INFO "CC|RX|TX|PACING|CONN"
.Ed
.Pp
Subscribe by keyword name to a user-space provider that has not started
yet; the filter takes effect when it registers:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
elog -c fanout_node VERBOSE "CHANNEL|SUBSCRIBER|OBJECT|STATS"
.Ed
.Pp
Subscribe to TCP events at INFO level with keywords as hex mask:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
elog -c tcp INFO 0x3F
.Ed
.Pp
Subscribe to TCP events at INFO level with all keywords except RX and TX:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
elog -c tcp INFO '^RX|^TX'
.Ed
.Pp
Subscribe to all TCP events including TRACE-level detail:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
elog -c tcp TRACE
.Ed
.Pp
Subscribe to multiple providers:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
elog -c tcp INFO -c hpts WARN
.Ed
.Pp
Capture a CPU stack trace (cpu, process, and symbols providers, with
dump-state and user-symbol resolution):
.Bd -literal -offset indent
elog -S cpu -o /tmp/cpu.elog
.Ed
.Pp
The same CPU capture plus TCP events:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
elog -S cpu -c tcp -o /tmp/cpu-tcp.elog
.Ed
.Pp
Capture interface events plus TCP SAMPLE / SAMPLE_HIST snapshots:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
elog -S net -o /tmp/net.elog
.Ed
.Pp
CPU and network presets together:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
elog -S cpu|net -o /tmp/cpu-net.elog
.Ed
.Pp
Subscribe using numeric levels:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
elog -c tcp 3 0x3F
.Ed
.Pp
Set buffer size to 4MB per CPU:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
elog -b 4M -c tcp
.Ed
.Pp
Write output to a file (binary format, decode blobs embedded by default so
the file decodes on any host):
.Bd -literal -offset indent
elog -c tcp -o /tmp/events.bin
.Ed
.Pp
Read binary file and convert to text:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
elog -r /tmp/events.bin
.Ed
.Pp
Capture without embedded decode blobs, for a smaller file when the live
kernel will remain available to decode it later:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
elog -N -c tcp -o /tmp/events.bin
.Ed
.Pp
Show relative timestamps from trace start:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
elog -t -c tcp
.Ed
.Pp
Show both relative and inter-event delta timestamps:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
elog -t --delta-time -c tcp
.Ed
.Pp
Write per-session files with TCP metadata naming:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
elog -o dir=/tmp/traces -f tcp -c tcp
.Ed
.Pp
Print statistics on exit:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
elog -s -c tcp
.Ed
.Pp
Run a fixed-duration capture, suitable for scripted use:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
elog --duration 30 -c tcp -c hpts -o /tmp/cap.elog
.Ed
.Pp
Continuously capture TCP events into hourly, size-capped, gzipped files for
a separate uploader to ship:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
elog -c tcp INFO -o dir=/d/eventlog --roll-time 1h --roll-size 400M --gzip
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr eventlog_user 3 ,
.Xr elog 5 ,
.Xr eventlog 9
.Sh HISTORY
The
.Nm
utility first appeared in
.Fx 16.0 .

